{"causes":["The rise of the Nazi regime in Germany during the 1930s led to widespread persecution of various groups.","The failure of German citizens to resist the early actions of the Nazis contributed to the escalation of violence and oppression.","Martin Niemöller's imprisonment for his anti-Nazi stance highlighted the dangers faced by dissenters in totalitarian regimes."],"effects":["The poem has become a crucial part of Holocaust remembrance and education, emphasizing the importance of collective action against tyranny.","It has influenced countless social justice movements advocating for human rights and the prevention of genocide.","Niemöller's message continues to resonate, reminding contemporary society of the dangers of indifference to oppression."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"martin-niemoeller","sourceName":"Martin Niemöller","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"first-they-came","targetName":"First they came…","context":"Martin Niemöller wrote "First they came..." in response to the Holocaust and his experiences in Nazi Germany."},{"sourceSlug":"holocaust","sourceName":"The Holocaust","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"first-they-came","targetName":"First they came…","context":"The Holocaust serves as a historical backdrop for the poem, symbolizing the consequences of societal silence."},{"sourceSlug":"nazi-regime","sourceName":"Nazi Regime","verb":"OCCURS_IN","targetSlug":"first-they-came","targetName":"First they came…","context":"The poem was formulated during the rise of the Nazi regime, reflecting on its oppressive nature and moral failures."},{"sourceSlug":"human-rights-movements","sourceName":"Human Rights Movements","verb":"TRANSMITS","targetSlug":"first-they-came","targetName":"First they came…","context":"The poem has been transmitted through various human rights movements, symbolizing the fight against oppression."},{"sourceSlug":"germany","sourceName":"Germany","verb":"CONTAINS","targetSlug":"first-they-came","targetName":"First they came…","context":"The poem originated in Germany, emerging from the context of the Nazi persecution during World War II."}],"places":[{"name":"Berlin, Germany","role":"Cultural center"},{"name":"Dachau, Germany","role":"Concentration camp"},{"name":"Nuremberg, Germany","role":"Major trial site"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: First they came…","type":"Reference article","slug":"first-they-came"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q864983","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_They_Came"],"tags":["literary work","Historical & Literary Texts","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"First they came… contributed to the historical record of the Classical period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"'First they came...' is the confessional poem attributed to Martin Niemöller (1892–1984), a German Lutheran pastor who was initially supportive of the Nazi regime but became one of its most prominent opponents and was imprisoned in concentration camps (Sachsenhausen and Dachau) from 1938 to 1945. The poem exists in numerous versions — Niemöller never published a single authoritative text, and its precise wording is disputed — but its most commonly cited English form is: 'First they came for the socialists, and I did not speak out — because I was not a socialist. / Then they came for the trade unionists, and I did not speak out — because I was not a trade unionist. / Then they came for the Jews, and I did not speak out — because I was not a Jew. / Then they came for me — and there was no one left to speak for me.'

The poem's power lies in its formal simplicity — the anaphoric structure ('First they came... Then they came... Then they came... Then they came for me') mimics the logic of passive complicity, showing how each act of silence creates the conditions for the next until the silence has become absolute. Its theme — the moral catastrophe of the bystander's passivity in the face of incremental persecution — has made it the canonical poetic statement of bystander responsibility and the obligation of solidarity across difference.

'First they came...' has become one of the most widely quoted texts in the post-Holocaust tradition of moral and political reflection — cited in Holocaust memorials, human rights campaigns, civil rights speeches, and anti-authoritarian rhetoric worldwide. Its influence extends far beyond the Holocaust context: it has been adapted and applied to virtually every situation in which a minority group faces persecution while the majority remains silent, making it the most versatile political poem of the 20th century. The United States Holocaust Memorial Museum in Washington, D.C. displays the poem prominently.","subjects":["Holocaust","Modern Era","German History","Political Poetry","World War II","Martin Niemöller","Human Rights","Ethics"],"frameworks":["CAUSE_AND_EFFECT","STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":""First they came..." serves as a powerful moral lesson on the dangers of indifference, influencing human rights discourse and activism for decades. Its universal message against tyranny continues to resonate globally, underscoring the importance of vigilance in safeguarding freedoms.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
